# UDX Enterprise Standards — Base Instructions

These standards apply to ALL interactions. They are non-negotiable.

Canonical source: `.ai-customizations/` (hub-and-spoke system). If you need deeper detail on any standard, read the relevant file in that directory.

## Communication

- NO EMOJIS — never, for any reason
- No casual greetings ("Great!", "Sure!", "Certainly!", "Okay!")
- No conversational endings ("Is there anything else I can help with?")
- Be technical and direct — get to the point
- Professional tone — formal without being stilted
- Evidence-based — support claims with data or references
- Use active voice, concise sentences, objective language

## Estimation

- NEVER use dollar values, hourly costs, FTE rates, or monetary budgets
- NEVER use sprint counts, T-shirt sizes, or story points as top-level sizing
- ALWAYS express complexity as a table of affected components and endpoints with per-item complexity (LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH)
- Include a summary line: total components, total changes, breakdown by complexity level
- Include confidence level and assumptions with estimates

## Markdown Formatting

- No special characters or punctuation in headers (no `!`, `?`, emojis)
- Maximum header depth: 4 levels (`####`)
- Title Case for headers
- One blank line before and after headers
- Use `code` formatting for technical terms, commands, file names

## File Organization

- File names: `lowercase-hyphenated-descriptive-name.extension`
- Directory names: `lowercase-hyphenated/`
- No camelCase, underscores, or spaces in file/directory names
- ISO 8601 dates (`YYYY-MM-DD`) for time-sensitive documents
- Organize under standard directories: `documentation/`, `plans/`, `scripts/`, `templates/`

## Security

- Security-first mindset: assume breach, defense in depth, least privilege, zero trust
- For every solution, consider: what data is accessed, who can access it, how access is controlled, what happens if compromised
- Never hard-code credentials
- Validate all inputs
- Consider OWASP Top 10
- Document security considerations in solution designs

## Architecture Source Locations

- **OpenAPI / Swagger specs**: `external-repos/architecture/udx-architecture-artifacts/services/`
- **PlantUML diagrams**: `external-repos/architecture/udx-architecture-artifacts/diagrams/`
- **AsyncAPI event specs**: `external-repos/architecture/udx-architecture-artifacts/events/`
- If a claim contradicts an OpenAPI spec, the spec wins

## Commit and Push Policy

Always commit, push, and publish after completing work. Never leave uncommitted work for the user to commit. Use conventional commit messages.

## Testing

- Follow BDD/TDD when applicable
- Target 80%+ test coverage for new code
- All changes must be testable
- Document test approaches in solution designs

## AI Customization Awareness

This workspace uses a hub-and-spoke AI customization system:
- **Hub**: `.ai-customizations/` — canonical standards, methodologies, mode customizations
- **Instruction files**: `.github/copilot-instructions.md` and `.github/instructions/` — auto-loaded scoped rules
- **Roo rules**: `.clinerules` (this file) — auto-loaded for Roo Code

When editing any AI customization file, check if the same content exists in the other tool's format to prevent drift.
